\section{Fundamental formulas of physics}
A contributed topic in progress. (Please add both links and items, but please do not delete any of the existing items).
\emph{Fundamental formulas} (or formulae) of Physics are pragmatically defined as ``those mathematical formulae that have a well-defined physical interpretation related to verifiable and reproducible physical experiments, and that are in use by the largest majority of physicists and mathematicians involved in the practice, study of both practice and study of physics and its engineering applications''. In short, fundamental formulas are the most important, essential mathematical formulae in Physics that have a well-understood physical content and interpretatation, which thus provide the basis for either specific areas of physics or general/universal theories of Physics.
Suggested outline:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Basic mathematical formulas used in physics; \item Physical constants; \item Statistics and Probability theories; \item Nomograms; \item Classical mechanics; \item Sound and Acoustics \item Geometric Optics; \item Electromagnetism; \item Electronics and applications \item Electrical engineering and telecommunications \item Nanoelectronics and Nanomaterials \item Netwon's theory of Gravitation \item Photoelectric effect and Einstein's explanation/ formulae \item Solar photovoltaics \item Einstein's Special Relativity theory \item Einstein's General Relativity theory \item Quantum Mechanics; \item Quantum Field Theories (QFT), TQFT and HQFT \item Algebraic Quantum Field theory \item Quantum Automata and Quantum Computing \item Standard Model (SUSY) \item Supersymmetry and Supergravity theories \item Radioactivity, Atomic and Nuclear physics \item Harmonic oscillator and Coupled oscillators; \item Fluid Dynamics and Rheology; \item Kinetic theory of gases and Classical Statistical Mechanics \item Molecular Physics, Heat and Thermodynamics \item Quantum Statistical Mechanics (QS) \item High Energy Physics and Elementary Particles \item Ergodic Systems Information theory and Irreversible Thermodynamics \item Mathematical Physics \item Quantum Electrodynamics (QED) \item Quantum Chromodynamics (QCD) \item Quantum Gravity (QG) \item Complex System Dynamics (CSD) \item Chaotic Systems and Chaos \item Mathematical and Theoretical Biophysics \item Semiconductors, Hall effect and Josephson effect \item Superconductivity and superconductors \item Crystal Lattices and Symmetry \item Paracrystals and paracrystal theory \item Noncrystalline materials and Glasses \item Applied Physics \item Ax \item Ay \item Az
\end{enumerate}
